A||N||2||DaveL||Balky ECM Connectors 87 SE 2.5 A/T DIS||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-25-2000||09:46 AM||||The only major problem I'm having now is the temp sensor circuit. I recently had an ECM blowout due to some kind of short in the system. The mech that replaced it blew the first ECM out because he didn't find the short, but he replaced it for free. Said he found a spot where the harnesss had hit a heat sheild. At any rate,he also had to replace the exhaust manifold to get a new o2 sensor in since the old manifold came apart when he removed the old o2 unit. It runs great! Usually... keeps flasing the serv eng soon light, codes 14 and 15. This tells me the ECM keeps thinking the engine is running completely cold or very hot. The light will flash on and stay on, with the idle kicking up to 1100 in drive, and the timing obviously retarded. I get really bad mileage when it is running in this mode. Then the light will go off, the idle and performance returns to normal. At other times it comes on, but makes no difference in the way it runs. No other codes, just 14 and 15 constantly. This one is really confusing. The mech says the ECM connector is wallowed out, causing an open in that circuit. I think there is also a short somewhere along the line on the same circuit. I see a total of six connectors between the ones in the firewall and the ones at the bottom of the ECM. Should these be replaced to eliminate any opens of that nature? And will I have to seperate the two wires coming from the temp sensor to the ECM from all the others in the harness to check for shorts? I'm sure I need a continuity tester. Any questions/advice appreciated. I hate to run on, so I'll run out.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||02-25-2000||01:18 PM||||I'm not sure what he means by wallowed out???<P>The first thing I would check is for a correct funtional sensor. If you somehow got an on/off switch instead of the correct sensor it could cause your symptoms. Using an Ohm meter measure the resistance of the sensor directly (unplug and measure at sensor). Warm the car up, turn it off and unplug and measure again. Check against the table below for correct (at least close) values.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html</A> <P>If the sensor checks out an alternating open/short circuit in the wiring sounds likely. Here's an idea to help locate it.<BR>- disconnect battery ground<BR>- disconnect ECM connectors<BR>- locate the 2 wires going to the temp sensor (see the link below for a partial wiring diagram)<BR>- Measure the resistance of the sensor thru the wiring (should be the same as measured at sensor)<BR>- Have some one watch for resistance to change as you go thru shaking/twisting/pushing/pulling all suspected wiring and connectors.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> <P><BR>Good Luck, David||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-25-2000||07:09 PM||||If the ECM connector is like many of the other interior GM connectors........<P>If the ECM connector's problem is that the plastic is damaged........<P>you should be able to shell the connector.<P>in other words you sould be able to get a good connector from another GM car, doesn't have to be Fiero, just use the same connectors, most GM ECMs use the same connectors.<P>Then you carfully take the shell apart and use it to replace your damaged one. I don't have one accessable to look at but I recall this can be done to the ECM. The terminals are held either by a plastic "wedge" in the connector or they are held by little tabs on the terminals. To release the tabs you slide a small screw driver or paperclip in next to the terminal. Just where the clip is varies some from one connector style to the next.<P>If someone has good pictures of the connector I can tell you how to get it apart. I need to see the side and back(wire side) and one square shot from the front (ECM opening)<P>It's posible a terminal is wrecked. that is repaired the same way as shell damage is.||||reg||1
